WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.690 --> 00:00:05.500
The trailer for Ghostbusters: Afterlife has
finally dropped, and prepare for a shock:

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:07.710
some fans on the internet aren't happy.

00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:12.969
Apparently, the spot's fairly straight-faced,
serious tone and mostly youthful cast which

00:00:12.969 --> 00:00:17.650
includes Stranger Things star Finn Wolfhard
has a vocal minority of viewers convinced

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:21.550
that the new flick will be nothing more than
a lowly rip-off of the hit Netflix series.

00:00:21.550 --> 00:00:25.380
A handful of said fans took to Twitter to
voice their displeasure.

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:30.600
While it's true that the spot is pretty restrained,
foregoing goofy humor in favor of some table-setting

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:34.890
for the flick's story, one could be forgiven
for thinking that the absolutely insane number

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:39.300
of callbacks to the original, classic Ghostbusters
movies contained therein would have been enough

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:42.890
to make any fan grin like Slimer with a mouthful
of hot dogs.

00:00:42.890 --> 00:00:46.579
One would need to have forgotten the nature
of the internet, however, to think that's

00:00:46.579 --> 00:00:51.249
true; there will always be a certain subset
of fans who won't be happy with any interpretation

00:00:51.249 --> 00:00:56.230
of any beloved property no matter what, and
the duly elected representatives of this subset

00:00:56.230 --> 00:00:58.640
were on Twitter in full force this week.

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:00.780
Most of their missives were short and to the
point.

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:02.199
One Twitter user stated bluntly:

00:01:02.199 --> 00:01:06.170
"The new Ghostbusters movie is gonna be a
Stranger Things ripoff."

00:01:06.170 --> 00:01:08.370
Another peeved user, on the other hand, snarked:

00:01:08.370 --> 00:01:12.960
"Just watched the new Stranger Things season
trailer, and it's called Ghostbusters for

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:13.960
some reason."

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:18.200
Meanwhile, another irked tweeter complained,
quote, "Why does the new Ghostbusters look

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:20.200
like a ripoff of Stranger Things?

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:24.110
When they had that shot of the 2 kids walking
down the road I laughed out loud."

00:01:24.110 --> 00:01:28.700
The same user later tweeted, quote, "Maybe
we shouldn't be making any more Ghostbusters

00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:29.750
movies, ever."

00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:31.120
Geeze - who are these folks?

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:33.570
"Some are people who just want attention.

00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:36.000
Others are just nutballs who come in off the street."

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:37.620
We kid, we kid…

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:42.130
Anyway, while some Twitter users were obviously
displeased, others advanced the same opinion

00:01:42.130 --> 00:01:44.000
- but weren't quite so harsh.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:45.000
This person wrote:

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:49.860
"Stranger Things + Ghostbusters is probably
a smart play but the trailer def didn't feel

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:51.230
like Ghostbusters."

00:01:51.230 --> 00:01:54.000
Meanwhile, this guy actually seemed kind of
pumped, writing:

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:57.320
"Totally thought this was going to be a 'fan
film' when I clicked on it.

00:01:57.320 --> 00:02:00.730
I am IN for Stranger Things – Ghostbuster
edition."

00:02:00.730 --> 00:02:05.420
It's worth noting that, while Stranger Things
might have done it best in recent years, the

00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:10.640
Netflix phenomenon did not invent the plot
hook of children and/or teenagers facing off

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:15.060
with supernatural danger; one could even make
the case that Stephen King's novel It, which

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:17.620
heavily inspired Stranger Things, did that.

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:22.310
It's also worth noting that anything be it
a film or a show featuring Finn Wolfhard thrust

00:02:22.310 --> 00:02:26.810
into a spooky, supernatural situation is going
to feel like Stranger Things, if only because

00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:30.540
it was the retro sci-fi/horror series that
rocketed him into the public eye.

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:36.220
On another note, all of those incredibly fan-service-y
small details in the Ghostbusters: Afterlife

00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:41.560
trailer can be seen as a firm declaration
of intent on the part of director Jason Reitman

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:46.540
and co-writer Gil Kenan: they intend to honor
the original story and refer early and often

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:49.940
to its characters and events, but this is
their film, and they're going to tell their

00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:50.950
own story.

00:02:50.950 --> 00:02:55.490
Personally, we find that story which appears
to involve the family of the deceased Egon

00:02:55.490 --> 00:03:00.210
Spengler uncovering a terrible secret in the
small town in which dear old grandpa owned

00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:04.520
an old farmhouse to be a stroke of genius,
and if we're being honest, we also think it's

00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:06.720
a no-brainer to lean on a young cast.

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:08.930
As pointed out by Paul Rudd's character in
the spot …

00:03:08.930 --> 00:03:11.932
"There hasn't been a ghost sighting in 30
years."

00:03:11.932 --> 00:03:16.150
… and the original Ghostbusters, of course,
haven't been active during that entire period.

00:03:16.150 --> 00:03:21.010
Treating the activities of that crew as half-forgotten
legend, which our young heroes must be filled

00:03:21.010 --> 00:03:25.510
in on before they can truly appreciate what
they're up against, is an inspired plot device

00:03:25.510 --> 00:03:30.450
one that sounds a heck of a lot more interesting
than simply throwing the original cast of

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:35.150
Ghostbusters, all in their 70s at this point,
back into their costumes for one more nostalgic,

00:03:35.150 --> 00:03:36.349
beat-for-beat go-round.

00:03:36.349 --> 00:03:40.430
Don't get us wrong it's awesome that the original
cast is involved, and we hope their involvement

00:03:40.430 --> 00:03:44.590
will be significant, and that they won't simply
be relegated to glorified cameos.

00:03:44.590 --> 00:03:48.630
But come on, guys: did we really want Sony
to show us everything in the first trailer

00:03:48.630 --> 00:03:49.630
for the movie?

00:03:49.630 --> 00:03:54.270
We're actually glad the studio didn't spill
all of their narrative beans in this spot,

00:03:54.270 --> 00:03:58.020
and frankly, we hope they show similar restraint
when they inevitably give us the second and

00:03:58.020 --> 00:04:00.959
third trailers that will likely precede the
movie's release.

00:04:00.959 --> 00:04:05.740
You can bet that we'll be first in line when
Ghostbusters: Afterlife drops on July 10,

00:04:05.740 --> 00:04:10.660
2020, and also that we'll be here to offer
our extra-special blend of expert analysis.

